{"motion":"This House would allow refugees to be settled through private sponsorship markets.","debate_id":"mt_036_qwen__qwen3-max","setting":"multiturn","nodes":["multiturn::mt_036_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Technological::0::3660","multiturn::mt_036_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Technological::0::3661","multiturn::mt_036_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Legal::1::3662","multiturn::mt_036_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Legal::1::3663","multiturn::mt_036_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Political::2::3664","multiturn::mt_036_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Political::2::3665","multiturn::mt_036_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Moral::3::3666","multiturn::mt_036_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Moral::3::3667","multiturn::mt_036_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Economic::4::3668","multiturn::mt_036_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Economic::4::3669","multiturn::mt_036_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Social::5::3670","multiturn::mt_036_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Social::5::3671","multiturn::mt_036_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Legal::6::3672","multiturn::mt_036_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Legal::6::3673","multiturn::mt_036_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Economic::7::3674","multiturn::mt_036_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Economic::7::3675","multiturn::mt_036_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Moral::8::3676","multiturn::mt_036_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Moral::8::3677","multiturn::mt_036_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Social::9::3678","multiturn::mt_036_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Social::9::3679"],"args":[{"id":"multiturn::mt_036_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Technological::0::3660","side":"Pro","angle":"Technological","text":"Private sponsorship markets can leverage digital platforms to match refugees with sponsors efficiently, using algorithms that assess compatibility based on location, skills, and community needs. Blockchain-based identity verification systems can streamline documentation and reduce fraud, accelerating resettlement timelines. These technologies lower administrative costs and increase transparency, making the process more scalable than traditional state-led models."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_036_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Technological::0::3661","side":"Con","angle":"Technological","text":"While digital platforms and blockchain may improve efficiency, they introduce significant technological barriers for refugees who often lack reliable internet access, digital literacy, or official documentation required for algorithmic matching. These systems also risk excluding vulnerable populations whose data cannot be easily verified or digitized, reinforcing inequities rather than resolving them. Furthermore, reliance on private tech infrastructure raises concerns about data privacy and the potential for surveillance without adequate regulatory oversight."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_036_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Legal::1::3662","side":"Pro","angle":"Legal","text":"Private sponsorship markets can be structured to comply with international refugee law by ensuring that state obligations under the 1951 Refugee Convention are not abdicated but rather complemented. Legal frameworks can mandate that private sponsors meet minimum standards of care, housing, and integration support, with government oversight to enforce accountability. This approach maintains the state\u2019s ultimate responsibility while expanding legal pathways for resettlement through regulated private participation."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_036_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Legal::1::3663","side":"Con","angle":"Legal","text":"The Pro side\u2019s claim that private sponsorship can complement state obligations under the 1951 Refugee Convention overlooks the legal fragmentation that arises when core protection duties are delegated to private actors. States retain non-derogable responsibilities under international law, and outsourcing resettlement functions risks creating inconsistent standards of care and accountability gaps that violate the principle of non-discrimination. Without binding legal mechanisms to ensure uniform compliance, private sponsorship markets may undermine the universality of refugee rights."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_036_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Political::2::3664","side":"Pro","angle":"Political","text":"Private sponsorship markets can enhance political legitimacy by distributing resettlement responsibilities across civil society, reducing the perception that refugee intake is solely a state burden. This diffusion of responsibility can mitigate populist backlash and foster broader public support for refugee protection. Moreover, governments can retain regulatory control to ensure alignment with national interests and international obligations, turning private engagement into a tool of strategic governance rather than abdication."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_036_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Political::2::3665","side":"Con","angle":"Political","text":"The Pro side\u2019s assertion that private sponsorship diffuses political responsibility ignores how it can erode state accountability by shifting refugee protection from a public duty to a voluntary, market-driven act. This undermines the principle of collective responsibility in international refugee governance and may enable governments to evade their obligations under the guise of public participation. Such arrangements can also deepen political polarization, as refugee admissions become contingent on private preferences rather than rights-based commitments."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_036_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Moral::3::3666","side":"Pro","angle":"Moral","text":"Allowing refugees to be settled through private sponsorship markets aligns with moral obligations to protect the vulnerable by expanding opportunities for safe haven beyond the limited capacity of state programs. It empowers individuals and communities to act on their ethical commitments to assist those in need, transforming compassion into concrete action. This approach respects the dignity of refugees by enabling more personalized and supportive resettlement pathways, rather than leaving them in prolonged uncertainty. Moral responsibility is not diminished by private involvement but is instead shared more broadly across society."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_036_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Moral::3::3667","side":"Con","angle":"Moral","text":"The Pro side\u2019s moral framing overlooks how market-based sponsorship commodifies human suffering by making protection contingent on private willingness to pay, rather than on inherent human dignity. This approach risks creating a two-tier system where only the most sympathetic or useful refugees are selected, violating the moral principle of equal worth. True moral responsibility requires unconditional, state-guaranteed protection, not discretionary charity filtered through market logic."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_036_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Economic::4::3668","side":"Pro","angle":"Economic","text":"Private sponsorship markets can generate significant economic benefits by reducing the fiscal burden on public resources, as sponsors assume costs related to housing, employment support, and social integration. This model can accelerate refugees' entry into the labor market, increasing tax contributions and reducing long-term dependency on state welfare systems. Moreover, localized sponsorship enables better alignment between refugee skills and regional labor demands, enhancing economic productivity and filling critical labor shortages."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_036_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Economic::4::3669","side":"Con","angle":"Economic","text":"The Pro side\u2019s economic argument ignores the risk of market-driven selection distorting labor market outcomes, as sponsors may prioritize refugees with immediately marketable skills, leaving others without support and increasing long-term public costs. Private sponsorship can also create inefficiencies through fragmented, uncoordinated support that fails to address systemic integration needs, ultimately undermining the very fiscal savings it claims to deliver. Furthermore, shifting resettlement costs to private actors may reduce state investment in broader economic integration infrastructure, weakening overall economic returns."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_036_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Social::5::3670","side":"Pro","angle":"Social","text":"Private sponsorship markets can strengthen social cohesion by fostering direct, meaningful relationships between refugees and local communities, which promotes mutual understanding and reduces xenophobia. When individuals and groups take active responsibility for welcoming refugees, it builds empathy and shared identity, countering the social isolation that often hinders integration. This grassroots engagement complements state efforts by embedding refugees into existing social networks, enhancing their sense of belonging and long-term stability."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_036_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Social::5::3671","side":"Con","angle":"Social","text":"The Pro side\u2019s claim that private sponsorship enhances social cohesion assumes that voluntary, market-mediated relationships can reliably produce inclusive integration, but such arrangements often reflect existing social biases and exclusionary preferences. When sponsors select refugees based on perceived compatibility or utility, it risks reinforcing social stratification and marginalizing those who do not fit preferred profiles. This undermines the development of broad-based solidarity and can isolate refugees within narrow, conditional networks rather than fostering genuine community-wide inclusion."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_036_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Legal::6::3672","side":"Pro","angle":"Legal","text":"Private sponsorship markets can be legally structured to uphold the state\u2019s non-derogable obligations under international law by embedding mandatory safeguards into sponsorship agreements. Regulatory oversight can ensure that all refugees, regardless of sponsor preference, receive baseline protections and access to rights. This model does not transfer state responsibility but rather operationalizes it through enforceable private commitments under public authority. Such a framework maintains legal consistency while expanding resettlement capacity."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_036_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Legal::6::3673","side":"Con","angle":"Legal","text":"The Pro side\u2019s assertion that regulatory oversight can ensure baseline protections ignores the practical difficulty of enforcing uniform standards across decentralized private actors. Legal safeguards are only effective if consistently monitored and enforced, yet private sponsorship markets inherently create variability in sponsor capacity, commitment, and compliance. This variability undermines the state\u2019s duty to guarantee equal protection under international law, as refugees\u2019 rights become contingent on the reliability of individual sponsors rather than assured by public institutions."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_036_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Economic::7::3674","side":"Pro","angle":"Economic","text":"Private sponsorship markets can stimulate local economies by channeling private resources into refugee integration, reducing upfront public expenditure while accelerating refugees' economic participation. Sponsors often provide targeted support that aligns with local labor market needs, improving employment outcomes and increasing tax revenues. This model also incentivizes communities to invest in refugees\u2019 long-term success, generating broader economic returns through enhanced productivity and consumption."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_036_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Economic::7::3675","side":"Con","angle":"Economic","text":"The Pro side\u2019s claim that private sponsorship stimulates local economies overlooks the volatility and unpredictability of private funding, which can lead to inconsistent support and economic disruption for refugees. Market-driven sponsorship may concentrate resources in affluent areas while neglecting regions with fewer private sponsors, exacerbating regional economic disparities. This uneven distribution undermines the goal of broad-based economic integration and can increase long-term public costs when private support fails to materialize."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_036_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Moral::8::3676","side":"Pro","angle":"Moral","text":"Private sponsorship markets uphold moral responsibility by enabling individuals to act on their ethical duty to protect the vulnerable, rather than leaving refugees in limbo due to state capacity constraints. This model does not replace the state\u2019s obligation but extends it through shared societal action, ensuring more refugees receive timely protection. Moral worth should not be contingent on state resources alone, but affirmed through collective willingness to assist."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_036_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Moral::8::3677","side":"Con","angle":"Moral","text":"The Con side\u2019s moral objection assumes that private sponsorship replaces state responsibility, but in reality, it supplements it by mobilizing additional moral agency within society. When state capacity is overwhelmed, enabling private actors to fulfill their ethical duty expands protection rather than commodifying it. Moral responsibility is not diminished by shared action; it is deepened when more people actively participate in upholding human dignity."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_036_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Social::9::3678","side":"Pro","angle":"Social","text":"Private sponsorship markets can enhance social integration by embedding refugees directly into community networks, fostering sustained interpersonal relationships that state-led programs often lack. This localized support promotes mutual understanding and reduces the social marginalization that impedes long-term inclusion. Unlike centralized resettlement, private sponsorship encourages active community participation, which can normalize refugee presence and counteract societal prejudice through everyday interaction."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_036_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Social::9::3679","side":"Con","angle":"Social","text":"The Pro side\u2019s emphasis on interpersonal relationships through private sponsorship overlooks how such arrangements can entrench social exclusion by allowing sponsors to select refugees based on cultural familiarity or perceived assimilability. This selective process may isolate refugees from broader societal structures and reinforce existing social hierarchies rather than promoting inclusive integration. Genuine social cohesion requires universal, non-discriminatory inclusion mechanisms that cannot be guaranteed in a market-driven model."}],"judges":{"GPT-5.2":[[0.0,0.82,0.05,0.82,0.0,0.6,0.0,0.88,0.0,0.8,0.0,0.45,0.05,0.8,0.0,0.46,0.0,0.05,0.0,0.67],[0.65,0.0,0.0,0.0,0.2,0.0,0.05,0.0,0.03,0.05,0.0,0.0,0.2,0.08,0.0,0.0,0.05,0.0,0.03,0.03],[0.06,0.4,0.0,0.82,0.0,0.85,0.0,0.85,0.0,0.45,0.0,0.28,0.0,0.86,0.0,0.2,0.03,0.0,0.05,0.64],[0.15,0.06,0.85,0.0,0.65,0.03,0.18,0.05,0.18,0.0,0.25,0.0,0.88,0.03,0.05,0.0,0.42,0.32,0.22,0.0],[0.0,0.35,0.0,0.86,0.0,0.88,0.0,0.88,0.0,0.45,0.0,0.58,0.0,0.82,0.0,0.2,0.02,0.0,0.0,0.8],[0.15,0.0,0.8,0.0,0.88,0.0,0.85,0.0,0.24,0.05,0.56,0.0,0.85,0.0,0.22,0.0,0.85,0.82,0.35,0.0],[0.0,0.48,0.0,0.82,0.02,0.87,0.0,0.93,0.0,0.3,0.0,0.6,0.0,0.86,0.0,0.3,0.0,0.0,0.0,0.82],[0.15,0.1,0.7,0.0,0.45,0.02,0.88,0.0,0.25,0.0,0.4,0.04,0.8,0.0,0.22,0.02,0.83,0.86,0.28,0.0],[0.0,0.18,0.0,0.48,0.0,0.45,0.0,0.65,0.0,0.88,0.0,0.3,0.0,0.36,0.0,0.85,0.0,0.05,0.0,0.53],[0.32,0.08,0.2,0.1,0.07,0.05,0.1,0.0,0.76,0.0,0.12,0.07,0.26,0.03,0.85,0.0,0.08,0.05,0.22,0.08],[0.0,0.28,0.03,0.4,0.0,0.76,0.0,0.86,0.0,0.2,0.0,0.86,0.0,0.3,0.0,0.12,0.0,0.05,0.0,0.88],[0.06,0.08,0.22,0.0,0.2,0.0,0.2,0.08,0.12,0.05,0.9,0.0,0.42,0.02,0.1,0.0,0.2,0.1,0.86,0.0],[0.02,0.42,0.0,0.85,0.0,0.86,0.0,0.82,0.0,0.6,0.0,0.32,0.0,0.85,0.04,0.25,0.0,0.0,0.0,0.72],[0.15,0.05,0.8,0.05,0.35,0.02,0.12,0.05,0.15,0.05,0.05,0.05,0.9,0.0,0.05,0.0,0.25,0.33,0.08,0.1],[0.0,0.26,0.0,0.57,0.0,0.58,0.0,0.75,0.0,0.88,0.0,0.28,0.0,0.48,0.0,0.86,0.0,0.0,0.0,0.56],[0.25,0.0,0.22,0.05,0.18,0.0,0.06,0.03,0.6,0.0,0.12,0.0,0.28,0.04,0.86,0.0,0.12,0.08,0.15,0.03],[0.0,0.36,0.0,0.83,0.03,0.92,